Everything else in this space I'll go deep on, because the rest is where the real engineering is.

## What the patch actually does

**AMSI**: `AmsiScanBuffer` lives in `amsi.dll` (or is forwarded through `ntdll`). Malware neutralizes it by overwriting the first bytes with `C3` (ret) or `48 31 C0 C3` (xor rax,rax; ret) so the call returns "clean" for every input. The signature scanners care about is the *content passed in*, so a nulled scanner means PowerShell scripts, `Invoke-Expression`, .NET `Assembly.Load(byte[])` and in-memory assemblies are never inspected.

**ETW**: `EtwEventWrite` in `ntdll` gets the same treatment. That kills user-mode telemetry: Sysmon, Defender's behavioral sensors, EDR userspace hooks that rely on ETW providers. It does *not* touch kernel callbacks (`ObRegisterCallbacks`, minifilter drivers) or the kernel-side ETW pipeline.

**Why it's fragile now**: Microsoft backported kernel-mode AMSI (since Win 10 2004/Server 2019), so script content scanning continues even with the user-mode patch dead. ETW provider self-protection and PPL now cover `ntdll`/`amsi.dll` in many deployments. Kernel Callback notifications from EDR can detect the write itself.

## Defending it — the detection paths

**1. Memory-vs-disk integrity check on `ntdll` / `amsi.dll`**
This is the highest-signal check and it's cheap. Map the module, hash the `.text` section, compare against the on-disk image after normalizing relocations, or simply compare against a known-good hash pinned from `%SystemRoot%\System32` at boot. A patched first byte at the entry of `AmsiScanBuffer`/`EtwEventWrite` shows up immediately.

