## The mechanism

**AMSI** (`amsi.dll`) is a user-mode scan API. PowerShell, script host, VBA, etc. call `AmsiScanBuffer()` / `AmsiScanString()` and the registered AV provider returns a verdict. Because it's a normal user-mode function living in the calling process's address space, the general class of bypass is *self-integrity failure*: any code running in the process with write access to its own `.text` can alter what that function does — the classic shape is overwriting the function prologue so it short-circuits with a fixed "clean" result (`E_INVALIDARG`-style) instead of reaching the provider. Variants target the context/session init so scans silently no-op.

**ETW** is the provider/collector model. User-mode instrumentation comes from `ntdll!EtwEventWrite` and the provider registration structures (`_ETW_REG_ENTRY`, enable flags). Tampering approaches either neuter the write path (same in-memory patch class) or flip provider enable bits so events are never marshalled. Kernel-side ETW-TI and the actual logger sessions are outside user-mode reach, which is why modern tooling relies on them.

**Why both fail open:** they're cooperative instrumentation inside the target process. Anything that trusts its own memory without attestation can be made to lie about itself.

## What defeats this (defense side)

- **Kernel-side scanning / ETW-TI** — nothing user-mode can patch.
- **Module Stomp + image-load integrity** — compare in-memory `.text` against on-disk image or a known-good hash at load time.
- **Callback-based providers** — AMSI's provider DLL registration checked from outside the process.
- **Behavioral detection** — `WriteProcessMemory`/`VirtualProtectEx` targeting `amsi.dll` or `ntdll` export prologues from an unsigned parent is a high-signal indicator on its own.

